# aether-stack-sentinel-and-monotone-ordering-invariants

Aether's evaluation stack maintains two structural invariants throughout file execution: (1) Sentinel — a zero is pushed onto the stack before the first line of any file is evaluated, and this sentinel is never popped, ensuring the stack is never truly empty during normal evaluation; (2) Monotone ordering — all values pushed onto the stack after the sentinel are strictly increasing from bottom to top at all times. Together these invariants guarantee that stack underflow cannot occur during normal evaluation and that the relative ordering between any two live stack entries is always deterministic and fully defined.

## Procedures
- When tracing Aether execution, initialize the stack with a single zero before processing any instruction.
- When verifying a push operation, confirm the value to be pushed is strictly greater than the current top; flag violations as runtime invariant errors.
- When writing stack-based tests for the Aether runtime, always seed the stack with the zero sentinel before the first evaluated line.

## Conventions
- The zero sentinel at the bottom is a structural constant of the evaluation environment, not a value pushed by the program under evaluation.
- Stack values form a strictly monotone increasing sequence: each pushed value must be greater than every value below it.

